    "id": "naked-nutrition-naked-egg",
    "name": "Naked Egg White Protein",
    "brand": "Naked Nutrition",
    "badge": "Best Dairy-Free Animal Protein",
    "category": "Egg white protein",
    "description": "Naked Egg is dried egg white and nothing else in the unflavored version, per the brand. A two-scoop serving of about 31 g delivers about 25 g of protein in about 110 calories, fat-free and lactose-free. It suits people who want a complete animal protein without milk, or who react to pea and soy as well as dairy.",
    "pros": [
      "Single ingredient in the unflavored version",
      "About 25 g of complete protein in about 110 calories",
      "Free of dairy, legumes and soy"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Premium price tier per 25 g of protein",
      "Plain, slightly eggy taste and can foam when shaken",
      "Not suitable for an egg allergy"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The dairy-free pick for people who want animal protein and no legumes.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein / serving": "About 25 g (2 scoops)",
      "Calories": "About 110",
      "Third-party tested": "No sport certification confirmed",
      "Best for": "Dairy and legume sensitivity"
    }
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Lactose-Free Protein Powder",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best lactose-free protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For people with lactose intolerance who tolerate dairy protein, a whey isolate such as Isopure Zero Carb, which is labeled lactose-free, is usually the best choice. For anyone who needs to avoid milk entirely, a dairy-free powder such as Garden of Life Sport, Vega Sport or Naked Pea is the better pick. The right answer depends on whether lactose or milk protein is the problem."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is whey protein isolate lactose-free?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most whey isolates are very low in lactose, typically well under a gram per scoop, but not all are labeled lactose-free. Isopure Zero Carb carries the claim; others such as ISO100 are simply low in carbohydrate and sugar. Most people with lactose intolerance tolerate isolate well, but very sensitive people may still react, so start with a single scoop in water."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best dairy-free protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For training, Garden of Life Sport and Vega Sport are strong dairy-free choices because they deliver about 30 g of protein and carry NSF Certified for Sport status per the brands. Naked Pea is the budget single-ingredient pick, and Naked Egg is the option for people who want animal protein without milk. Check the allergen statement if your sensitivity is severe."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best non-dairy protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Treat non-dairy as a marketing phrase and look for products with no milk ingredients at all. Plant blends such as Vega Sport and Garden of Life Sport, single-source pea isolates like Naked Pea, and egg white powders all qualify. A vegan label is the simplest way to confirm there is no milk-derived ingredient in the tub."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I take lactase with protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Over-the-counter lactase tablets or drops can help you tolerate a whey concentrate, casein or a blend containing milk. Take the enzyme with the first sip so it works while the lactose passes through. Lactase does not help with a milk allergy, and it will not fix symptoms caused by sugar alcohols, gums or added fiber."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does lactose-free protein powder still contain milk?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Lactose-free whey and casein products still contain milk proteins, and the allergen statement will say contains milk. Only a dairy-free product, such as a plant or egg white powder, is free of milk. If you have a diagnosed milk allergy, talk to your allergist or doctor about which products and facilities are safe for you."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is casein protein lactose-free?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Casein powders are filtered and fairly low in lactose, often a few grams of carbohydrate per serving, but they are not as low as whey isolate and are rarely labeled lactose-free. Many lactose-intolerant people tolerate a normal serving, especially with a lactase tablet. If you are very sensitive, an isolate or a dairy-free powder is the safer starting point."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Specialty Protein",
    tags: [
      "best lactose free protein powder",
      "best dairy free protein powder",
      "best non dairy protein powder",
      "lactose free whey isolate",
      "lactase"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Lactose-Free Protein Powder</Fragment>
      Eight powders for people who cannot handle lactose, split into the two groups that matter: whey isolates that keep lactose close to zero, and dairy-free powders with no milk in them at all.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#quick-answer">Quick Comparison</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#lactose-vs-dairy-free">Lactose-Free vs Dairy-Free</a>
      <a href="#isolate-lactose">Lactose in Whey Isolate</a>
      <a href="#which-labels">Which Labels Say Lactose-Free</a>
      <a href="#lactase">Lactase and Treated Products</a>
      <a href="#non-dairy-options">Non-Dairy Options by Source</a>
      <a href="#how-we-picked">How We Picked</a>
      <a href="#choosing">Choosing by Sensitivity</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          If lactose is your only problem, a whey protein isolate is usually the best lactose-free protein powder you can buy: it keeps whey's taste and muscle-building profile while filtering most of the milk sugar out, and a few, like Isopure Zero Carb, say lactose-free on the label. If you have a milk allergy, avoid dairy for other reasons, or react even to isolate, skip whey entirely and choose a dairy-free powder made from pea, a plant blend or egg white. Those are different problems with different answers, and most of the mistakes people make in this aisle come from treating them as one.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="quick-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Quick Answer: Lactose-Free Picks Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here is the short version before the detail. If you tolerate dairy protein but not milk sugar, start with Isopure Zero Carb, which is labeled lactose-free, or Transparent Labs Grass-Fed Whey Isolate if you want an independent certification and no artificial sweetener. If you need no dairy at all, Garden of Life Sport and Vega Sport are the certified plant options for training, Naked Pea is the budget single-ingredient pick, and Naked Egg is the choice for people who want an animal protein without milk.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sugar</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Price tier</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Certification</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Isopure Zero Carb</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 100-110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check certifier databases</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Transparent Labs Whey Isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">28 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120-130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Premium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Informed Choice (brand)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dymatize ISO100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 110-120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Banned-substance tested (brand)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Garden of Life Sport Plant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 170-180</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No added sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Premium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">NSF Certified for Sport (brand)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Vega Sport Premium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 160-170</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Premium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">NSF Certified for Sport (brand)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Orgain Organic Plant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0-1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">USDA Organic</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Naked Pea</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 27 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unsweetened</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None confirmed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Naked Egg</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Unsweetened</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Premium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None confirmed</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            All figures are per the label at the time of writing and vary by flavor and serving size. Two scoops make a serving for some of the plant powders, which is why their calories run higher. Certification status changes, so look up the exact product and flavor in the NSF or Informed Choice database before you rely on it. Price tier reflects typical cost per 25 g of protein at mainstream retailers, not the sticker price of the tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more thing worth knowing up front: none of the three whey isolates is safe for a milk allergy. They are low in lactose, the sugar, but they are made of milk protein, which is what the immune system reacts to. If that is your situation, go straight to picks four through eight.
          </p>
        </div>

