            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best lactose-free protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For people with lactose intolerance who tolerate dairy protein, a whey isolate such as Isopure Zero Carb, which is labeled lactose-free, is usually the best choice. For anyone who needs to avoid milk entirely, a dairy-free powder such as Garden of Life Sport, Vega Sport or Naked Pea is the better pick. The right answer depends on whether lactose or milk protein is the problem.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is whey protein isolate lactose-free?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most whey isolates are very low in lactose, typically well under a gram per scoop, but not all are labeled lactose-free. Isopure Zero Carb carries the claim; others such as ISO100 are simply low in carbohydrate and sugar. Most people with lactose intolerance tolerate isolate well, but very sensitive people may still react, so start with a single scoop in water.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best dairy-free protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For training, Garden of Life Sport and Vega Sport are strong dairy-free choices because they deliver about 30 g of protein and carry NSF Certified for Sport status per the brands. Naked Pea is the budget single-ingredient pick, and Naked Egg is the option for people who want animal protein without milk. Check the allergen statement if your sensitivity is severe.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best non-dairy protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Treat non-dairy as a marketing phrase and look for products with no milk ingredients at all. Plant blends such as Vega Sport and Garden of Life Sport, single-source pea isolates like Naked Pea, and egg white powders all qualify. A vegan label is the simplest way to confirm there is no milk-derived ingredient in the tub.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can I take lactase with protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Over-the-counter lactase tablets or drops can help you tolerate a whey concentrate, casein or a blend containing milk. Take the enzyme with the first sip so it works while the lactose passes through. Lactase does not help with a milk allergy, and it will not fix symptoms caused by sugar alcohols, gums or added fiber.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does lactose-free protein powder still contain milk?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Lactose-free whey and casein products still contain milk proteins, and the allergen statement will say contains milk. Only a dairy-free product, such as a plant or egg white powder, is free of milk. If you have a diagnosed milk allergy, talk to your allergist or doctor about which products and facilities are safe for you.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is casein protein lactose-free?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Casein powders are filtered and fairly low in lactose, often a few grams of carbohydrate per serving, but they are not as low as whey isolate and are rarely labeled lactose-free. Many lactose-intolerant people tolerate a normal serving, especially with a lactase tablet. If you are very sensitive, an isolate or a dairy-free powder is the safer starting point.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

